    tags: ["Professional Services", "Law Firms", "Accounting", "Consulting", "RevOps", "CRM"],
    body: [
      { t: "p", v: "Professional services firms, whether in law, accounting, financial advisory, management consulting, or any other knowledge-based discipline, grow differently from product companies. There are rarely cold outbound campaigns or paid ad funnels. Growth comes from reputation, referrals, and existing client relationships that deepen over time." },
      { t: "p", v: "That growth model has a critical vulnerability: it is entirely dependent on people. If the partner who manages a key relationship leaves, takes the relationship with them. If a referral comes in while the right person is on vacation, it gets lost. If a past client has not heard from you in 18 months, they have already moved to someone else." },
      { t: "p", v: "Revenue operations solves this by making the relationship management systematic. The referrals get captured. The leads get followed up. The client relationships stay warm even when the team is busy. None of it requires a dedicated sales team." },
      { t: "h2", v: "The specific RevOps challenges in professional services" },
      { t: "ul", v: [
        "Referrals that come in through personal relationships are not logged anywhere and get forgotten",
        "New enquiries wait days or weeks for a response because the billable team is too busy",
        "No structured process for following up on proposals that have been sent but not yet accepted",
        "Past clients who could bring repeat business or referrals have not been contacted in years",
        "There is no system for tracking which referral sources are most valuable and investing in those relationships",
        "Partner knowledge about client relationships exists only in that partner's head",
      ]},
      { t: "h2", v: "What RevOps builds for a professional services firm" },
      { t: "h3", v: "Referral tracking and source attribution" },
      { t: "p", v: "Every new enquiry should be tagged with how it arrived: which referral partner sent it, which piece of content drove it, which event it came from. Over 12 months, that data tells you which referral relationships are producing the most valuable clients and which networking events deserve your time. That is the information that should drive where you spend your business development hours." },
      { t: "h3", v: "Enquiry response and proposal follow-up" },
      { t: "p", v: "When a potential client reaches out, an immediate acknowledgement sets a professional tone and buys time for the right person to respond. A RevOps-configured workflow routes the enquiry, sends that acknowledgement automatically, and creates a task for a human follow-up within a defined window. Proposals that have been sent and not responded to receive a structured follow-up sequence rather than waiting for the partner to remember." },
      { t: "h3", v: "Client relationship maintenance" },
      { t: "p", v: "A client who completed a matter six months ago and has not heard from you is at risk of finding someone else when the next need arises. A RevOps system tags clients by service type, matter completion date, and estimated next need, then triggers timely touchpoints: a relevant article, a check-in call prompt, a seasonal newsletter, or a referral request at the right moment." },
      { t: "h3", v: "Alumni and referral partner management" },
      { t: "p", v: "Former colleagues, former clients, and strategic referral partners are often a professional services firm's most valuable external network. A CRM with a dedicated segment for these relationships, with scheduled touchpoints and a record of every interaction, keeps those relationships active without relying on personal memory." },
      { t: "h2", v: "The tools that work best for professional services" },
      { t: "p", v: "Professional services firms typically need a lighter CRM than a complex enterprise sales team. HubSpot, Clio (for law firms), and Pipedrive all work well in this sector. The key is not choosing the most powerful tool. It is choosing the tool your team will actually use, configured simply enough that logging an interaction takes seconds rather than minutes." },
      { t: "quote", v: "In professional services, the relationship is the product. Every system you build should make it easier to be reliably present for the people who matter most to your firm's growth." },
      { t: "callout", v: "A useful exercise: list your top 20 clients from the last three years and check when each was last contacted by someone at your firm. The gaps in that list are your immediate business development opportunity." },
      { t: "cta", v: "SuperTelque builds CRM and RevOps systems for professional services firms, consultancies, and B2B businesses across the US. We design referral pipelines, client retention workflows, and the business development infrastructure that keeps your firm growing.", href: "/services", label: "See our CRM service" },
    ],
